course: dictionary
lesson: 12890
title: Saying words clearly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To enunciate means to say words clearly and carefully, so that each sound can be heard. Actors, readers and speakers practise it so that the people listening can follow every word. «Please enunciate each word so the back row can hear you.» Here, enunciate means speaking so clearly that nobody has to guess what you said.
explain: Enunciate can also be a formal word. It can mean to state an idea, plan or belief clearly. «The head teacher will enunciate the new rules at assembly.» She is not talking about her mouth here. She is saying the rules clearly and openly so everyone knows them.
explain: When you meet enunciate, check the words around it. Is someone speaking or reading aloud? Then it probably means saying words clearly. Is someone presenting a plan or a belief? Then it probably means stating it clearly. Try swapping in a simple word such as say clearly or state clearly and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Enunciate is a verb, so its forms are enunciate, enunciates, enunciated and enunciating.
fact: «Enunciate» means to say words clearly, sounding each part.
fact: In a formal sense, «enunciate» means to state an idea, plan or belief clearly.
fact: Common phrases are «enunciate clearly» and «enunciate each word».
fact: Actors, readers and speakers practise enunciating so that their audience can hear and understand them.
exercise: «Please enunciate each word so the back row can hear you.» What does enunciate mean here? | Shout the words without stopping | *Say words clearly, sounding each part | Write the words down in a notebook | Say words very quietly
exercise: Priya's drama teacher says, «Enunciate your lines for the audience.» What should Priya do? | Read the lines silently to herself | Skip the lines she finds hard | Mumble and look at the floor | *Say each line clearly so everyone can hear it
exercise: Read this: «The leader will enunciate the new policy at the meeting.» Find and copy the word that means to state something clearly. | policy | leader | *enunciate | meeting
test: «Speak slowly and enunciate, so that every sound is heard.» What does enunciate mean? | *Say sounds clearly | Whisper in a secret voice | Forget the words you planned | Hum a quiet tune
test: Which speaker best shows how to enunciate? | A speaker who turns away from the audience | *A speaker whose words are clear and easy to follow | A speaker who reads with their eyes closed | A speaker who talks so fast that nobody can catch the words
exam: «The minister will enunciate the principle of kindness in her talk.» In this sentence, what does enunciate mean? | *To state an idea clearly | To eat something slowly | To sing very softly | To run away from a problem
